        <div id="what-is-recomp" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is Body Recomposition, and Is It Real?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Body recomposition means losing fat and gaining muscle at the same time, so your body changes shape even if the scale barely moves. The traditional advice was that you cannot do both at once: you bulk in a surplus to build muscle, then cut in a deficit to lose fat. That remains the most efficient route for many lean, experienced lifters, but recomposition is real and well documented in the research, especially in certain groups.
          </p>

          <p>
            The clearest controlled example is a 2016 study by Longland and colleagues in the American Journal of Clinical Nutrition. Young men ate about 40 percent fewer calories than they needed for four weeks while doing intense resistance and interval training six days a week. The group eating 2.4 g of protein per kilogram a day gained about 1.2 kg of lean mass and lost about 4.8 kg of fat; the group eating 1.2 g/kg kept lean mass roughly unchanged and lost about 3.5 kg of fat.
          </p>

          <p>
            That study is often quoted as proof that high protein builds muscle in a deficit, and it does support that, but notice the conditions: a supervised, very demanding program in young men who were not highly trained. It shows what is possible, not what a typical person should expect from adding a shake. A 2020 review in the Strength and Conditioning Journal by Barakat and colleagues went further and argued that recomposition also happens in trained lifters, with training quality, protein intake and other lifestyle factors as the levers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to tell if it is working</h3>

          <p>
            Because fat loss and muscle gain partly cancel out on the scale, body weight is a poor guide. Track waist measurement, progress photos in the same light, and, most important, whether your strength in key lifts is rising. Strength going up while the waist comes down is the signature of recomposition. Bathroom body-fat scales are too noisy to rely on week to week, and even DEXA scans have a margin of error that can swamp small changes.
          </p>
        </div>

