            Beginners are the clearest case. People new to resistance training respond quickly, and the stimulus of lifting is strong enough that they can build muscle even while eating somewhat below maintenance, provided protein is high. If you have never trained consistently, a recomposition approach, with modest calorie reduction, a structured lifting program and enough protein, is usually a better plan than either bulking or crash dieting.
          </p>

          <p>
            People returning after a break are the second group. Muscle that was once built tends to come back faster than it was first gained, an effect often attributed to changes that persist in muscle cells after training stops. Someone who lifted for years, stopped and gained some fat often sees fast recomposition in the first months back, which is why returning lifters frequently report losing their gut and getting stronger at the same time.
          </p>

          <p>
            People carrying more body fat also recompose more readily, because there is plenty of stored energy available to fund muscle growth while in a deficit. That is part of why many weight-loss studies with resistance training show lean mass held or gained while fat falls. The same principle applies, with medical guidance, to people losing weight on GLP-1 medications, where protecting muscle is a priority; our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-glp-1/">best protein powder for GLP-1</a> guide covers that.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lean, experienced lifters</h3>

