          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Much Protein Do You Need for Body Recomposition?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most-cited evidence is a 2018 meta-analysis by Morton and colleagues in the British Journal of Sports Medicine, covering 49 trials and about 1,800 participants doing resistance training. Protein supplementation increased gains in lean mass and strength, and the benefit for lean mass leveled off at around 1.6 g of protein per kilogram of body weight per day, with the statistical uncertainty extending up to about 2.2 g/kg. That is where the common 1.6 to 2.2 g/kg recommendation comes from.
          </p>

          <p>
            For recomposition specifically, aiming toward the upper half of that range makes sense, because a calorie deficit raises protein needs. The International Society of Sports Nutrition's position stand recommends 1.4 to 2.0 g/kg a day for most exercising people and notes higher intakes may help preserve lean mass during calorie restriction. A frequently cited 2014 review by Helms and colleagues suggested 2.3 to 3.1 g per kilogram of lean mass for lean, resistance-trained people dieting, which works out to roughly 2.0 to 2.7 g/kg of total body weight for someone at moderate body fat.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Body weight</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">1.6 g/kg per day</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">2.2 g/kg per day</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per meal at 0.4 g/kg (4 meals)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
