                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">112 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">154 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 28 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">80 kg (176 lb)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">128 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">176 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 32 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">90 kg (198 lb)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">144 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">198 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 36 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">100 kg (220 lb)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">160 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">220 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 40 g</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you carry a lot of body fat, base the calculation on a goal or reference body weight rather than your current weight, or the target becomes unrealistically high. Spreading protein across three or four meals of roughly 0.4 g/kg each, as Schoenfeld and Aragon suggested in a 2018 paper, is a sensible way to organize it. Our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> and <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a> guide help you set a number.
          </p>

          <p>
            These intakes are considered safe for healthy adults, but they are not for everyone. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant, or have a medical condition or take medication that affects protein or fluid needs, talk to your doctor or a registered dietitian before raising protein substantially. Protein powder is a convenience food: whole-food protein counts exactly the same toward the target.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="calories-training" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Calories and Training Matter More Than the Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein is necessary for recomposition but nowhere near sufficient. The driver of muscle growth is resistance training with progressive overload, meaning you gradually lift more weight or do more reps over weeks. Without that stimulus, extra protein in a deficit mostly helps you hold on to the muscle you have. A sensible program trains each major muscle group about twice a week, with sets taken close to failure, and tracks numbers so progress is visible.
          </p>

          <p>
            The calorie deficit should be modest. Very aggressive deficits, like the one in the Longland study, can work under supervision, but for most people a deficit of roughly 10 to 20 percent below maintenance, or simply eating at maintenance with high protein, makes recomposition far more sustainable and protects training quality. Losing about 0.5 percent of body weight a week or less is a reasonable pace when the aim is to keep or build muscle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sleep is the underrated third factor. Short sleep during a diet has been linked in controlled studies to more of the weight lost coming from lean mass rather than fat, and it makes training and appetite harder to manage. Daily steps and overall activity also make the deficit easier without cutting food further. These basics account for far more of your result than any choice between the powders on this page.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where a powder does make a difference is adherence. Hitting 150 g of protein a day from food alone is hard for many people, particularly on a budget or a busy schedule. A 120-calorie scoop that supplies about a fifth of the day's target, costs around a dollar or two, and takes thirty seconds to make is the practical reason to buy one. Our <a href="/do-protein-supplements-build-muscle/">do protein supplements build muscle</a> guide reviews what the research shows supplements add on top of diet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="creatine-casein-timing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Do Creatine, Casein or Protein Timing Help Recomposition?
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Creatine: the one add-on with strong evidence</h3>

          <p>
            Creatine monohydrate is the most researched sports supplement after protein, and meta-analyses consistently find that, combined with resistance training, it produces greater gains in lean mass and strength than training alone. The International Society of Sports Nutrition considers it safe and effective for healthy people at typical doses of 3 to 5 g a day. Some of the early lean-mass gain is water in muscle, so the scale may tick up slightly at first. If you want one supplement besides protein, this is it; our <a href="/best-protein-powder-with-creatine/">best protein powder with creatine</a> guide compares combined products.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Casein before bed</h3>

          <p>
            Casein digests slowly, and studies from Luc van Loon's group in the Netherlands found that about 40 g of casein before sleep increased overnight muscle protein synthesis, and that a pre-sleep protein supplement combined with 12 weeks of training increased gains in strength and muscle size compared with placebo. The effect is real but modest, and it largely works by adding protein to the day. It is a good option if evenings are when you fall short of your target; see our <a href="/best-protein-powder-before-bed/">best protein powder before bed</a> guide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Timing is a minor factor</h3>

          <p>
            The idea of a narrow anabolic window after training has faded. A 2013 meta-analysis by Schoenfeld, Aragon and Krieger found that the apparent benefit of protein timing largely disappeared once total daily protein was accounted for. Having a shake within a few hours of training is perfectly sensible, but there is no need to rush one down in the locker room. Total daily protein and its spread across meals matter far more; our <a href="/when-to-take-protein-powder/">when to take protein powder</a> guide covers timing in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hype" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Recomposition Claims to Ignore
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fat-burning protein powders are the first. Products marketed for cutting or shredding often add green tea extract, caffeine, L-carnitine or CLA to an otherwise ordinary whey. The doses are usually too small to matter, the evidence for most of these ingredients is weak, and stimulants add risk for some people. You pay extra for little. Our <a href="/best-fat-burning-protein-powder/">best fat burning protein powder</a> guide explains why a plain isolate usually beats them.
          </p>

          <p>
            BCAA supplements are the second. Branched-chain amino acids are already present in every complete protein, and whey is especially rich in them. Once you are eating enough total protein, research has not shown that extra BCAAs add muscle. They can make sense in narrow situations, but for recomposition they are mostly an expensive way to drink flavored water. Spend the money on more whey or on creatine instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third, be wary of dramatic before-and-after claims, especially those promising large muscle gains and large fat loss in a few weeks. Real recomposition in a trained person might mean a few pounds of muscle and a few pounds of fat over several months. Photos can be shaped by lighting, pump, posture, tanning and, in some cases, undisclosed drug use. If a product's marketing leans on transformations rather than label numbers and certifications, treat that as a reason for caution.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, ignore any suggestion that more protein is always better. Beyond roughly 2.2 g/kg, evidence for further muscle gain is thin for most people, and very high intakes crowd out the carbohydrates that fuel hard training and the fiber and plants that support overall health. Very high protein intakes have generally been well tolerated in healthy adults in studies, but they are not a shortcut. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a> guide covers who should be cautious.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-on-amazon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Buying Protein Powder for Recomposition on Amazon: Value and Verification
          </h2>

          <p>
            Recomposition is a months-long project, so you will likely buy several tubs, and the big names on this list are among the most counterfeited supplements on Amazon. Under the Add to Cart button, check Ships from and Sold by: you want the brand's own store or Amazon itself. Third-party sellers are not always a problem, but they raise the risk of fakes, heat-damaged stock and short dates. Be suspicious of a price well below the brand's other listings.
          </p>

          <p>
            When the tub arrives, check that the seal is intact, the label print is crisp, and a lot number and best-by date are printed on the tub. For certified products, look up the lot or flavor on the certifier's site, such as Informed Choice or NSF Certified for Sport, rather than trusting a logo in the listing images. Compare the Nutrition Facts on the tub with the label image in the listing: the images and bullet copy are not always updated after a reformulation, and the tub is the version that counts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Size traps and cost per 25 g</h3>

          <p>
            Big tubs are where the value is, but Amazon listings often show a price for a different size from the one pictured, and flavors in the same listing can be priced very differently. Confirm the weight and flavor selected before comparing. Then work out cost per 25 g of protein: price divided by servings, divided by protein per serving, multiplied by 25. A 5 lb tub of a mainstream whey commonly comes in well under a premium isolate on this measure. Our <a href="/tools/protein-cost-calculator/">protein cost calculator</a> does the arithmetic.
          </p>

          <p>
            Subscribe and Save typically takes 5 percent off, rising to up to 15 percent once you have five or more subscriptions in a month, which suits a powder you will use daily for months. Set the interval to match how fast you go through a tub at two scoops a day rather than accepting the default. Our <a href="/amazon-protein-powder/">Amazon protein powder</a> guide covers more listing traps, and <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">best NSF certified protein powder</a> lists certified options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We looked for powders sold on Amazon US that make a high daily protein target easy and affordable: complete proteins with good leucine content, at least about 18 g of protein per 100 calories, and a realistic cost per 25 g for daily use over months. We gave weight to third-party certification as listed by the brand, and to products that fill a specific recomposition need, such as a casein for the evening or a plant protein with creatine.
          </p>

          <p>
            We ranked from label analysis, certifications and published research, not hands-on testing; taste and mixing notes reflect what buyers report. We left out products whose main selling points are fat-burning additives or proprietary blends. Our <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> page explains how we weigh evidence, and our sibling guide to the <a href="/best-low-sodium-protein-powder/">best low sodium protein powder</a> covers several of the same isolates from another angle.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting the powder to do the work</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                No protein powder builds muscle or burns fat on its own. Recomposition comes from progressive resistance training, a modest deficit and enough total protein. The powder only makes the protein target easier and cheaper to hit, so plan training first and buy second.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting calories too hard</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A large deficit can work in a supervised study, but for most people it hurts training, sleep and adherence. A deficit of about 10 to 20 percent, or eating at maintenance with high protein, makes recomposition far more sustainable and protects muscle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging progress by the scale</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fat loss and muscle gain partly cancel out on the scale, so weight may barely move while your body changes. Track waist measurement, photos and strength in key lifts. Rising strength with a shrinking waist means it is working.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overshooting protein and undershooting carbs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Beyond about 2.2 g/kg, evidence for extra muscle gain is thin for most people. Pushing protein far higher can crowd out the carbohydrate that fuels hard training. Hit your target, then fill the rest of your calories with carbohydrate, fat and plenty of plants.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for fat-burning extras</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cutting or shredding formulas often add caffeine, green tea extract or carnitine in small doses with weak evidence. A plain whey plus 3 to 5 g of creatine monohydrate covers what the research supports, usually for less money.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best protein powder for body recomposition?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most people, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Whey, at about 24 g of protein for 120 calories per the label, offers the best mix of quality, certification per the brand and price. Dymatize ISO100 and Transparent Labs suit tighter calorie budgets, and Orgain's protein plus creatine is the best plant-based pick.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein do I need for body recomposition?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Aim for about 1.6 to 2.2 g of protein per kilogram of body weight a day, based on a 2018 meta-analysis by Morton and colleagues. In a calorie deficit, the upper half of that range is sensible. If you carry a lot of body fat, base the number on a goal weight. Spread it across three or four meals.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can you lose fat and build muscle at the same time?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Recomposition is well documented, especially in beginners, people returning to training, and those with more body fat. It requires progressive resistance training, a modest calorie deficit or maintenance calories, and high protein. Lean, experienced lifters can recompose too, but progress is slow and harder to see.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is whey or plant protein better for recomposition?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Whey has a slight edge because it is higher in leucine and digests quickly, but plant protein works if you eat enough of it. A pea and rice blend at a slightly higher dose, such as Orgain's 30 g serving, closes most of the gap. Total daily protein matters more than the source.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Should I take creatine for body recomposition?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Creatine monohydrate is the one supplement besides protein with strong evidence. Combined with resistance training, it consistently increases lean mass and strength in studies. A dose of 3 to 5 g a day is typical. Talk to a doctor first if you have kidney disease or another medical condition.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>When should I drink a protein shake for recomposition?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Whenever it helps you reach your daily target. Research suggests timing matters far less than total protein, so a shake within a few hours of training, at breakfast or in the evening all work. Casein before bed is a reasonable option if evenings are where you fall short.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How long does body recomposition take?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Beginners and returning lifters often see clear changes in strength, waist measurement and photos within two to three months. Lean, experienced lifters may need six months or more to see a meaningful change. Weekly scale weight can stay flat throughout, so track strength and measurements instead.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

